        {
          "id": "java-string-eq",
          "title": "The login check that never matches",
          "situation": "A username comparison with == works for literals in unit tests but fails for input read from the console.",
          "prompt": "Why does == fail, and what is the correct comparison?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Login.java",
              "String input = scanner.nextLine();\nif (input == \"admin\") { grant(); }  // false for runtime strings"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "== compares object references \u2014 use input.equals(\"admin\"); literals may be interned, which is why tests accidentally passed",
            "Strings from I/O are char[] \u2014 convert with new String(input) first",
            "== works for strings but not for scanner input \u2014 call scanner.next() instead",
            "String comparison requires compareTo \u2014 equals() only works for enums"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "== on objects tests reference identity. String literals are interned (one shared instance per distinct literal), so == can appear to work in tests, but a runtime-built string is a different object with equal content. equals() compares content \u2014 always use it for value comparison. (For null-safety, \"admin\".equals(input) avoids NPE.)",
          "hint": "What does == compare for objects \u2014 the characters, or which objects they are?",
          "objectives": [
            "String equality",
            "equals vs ==",
            "Interning"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "java-int-division",
          "title": "The percentage that is always zero",
          "situation": "A progress reporter computes done/total*100 as ints and always displays 0% until the very end.",
          "prompt": "Why is the percentage zero, and how do you fix it?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Progress.java",
              "int pct = done / total * 100;  // done < total -> 0 * 100 = 0"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "done/total is integer division, truncating to 0 before the multiplication \u2014 cast first: (int)(done * 100.0 / total)",
            "int arithmetic overflows at 100 \u2014 use long for pct",
            "Multiplication must come first textually \u2014 write 100 * done / total as int math",
            "The expression is evaluated right-to-left \u2014 add parentheses around done/total"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "With int operands, done/total truncates toward zero \u2014 any proper fraction becomes 0, and 0*100 stays 0. Promoting one operand to double before dividing (done * 100.0 / total) performs floating-point division; cast back to int for display. (100 * done / total also works in int math but risks overflow for huge values.)",
          "hint": "What is the value of done/total before the *100 ever runs?",
          "objectives": [
            "Integer division",
            "Numeric promotion"
          ],
          "mins": 4
        },
        {
          "id": "java-switch-expr",
          "title": "The switch that must produce a value",
          "situation": "A refactored method needs a switch to yield a value. The old statement-switch with a mutable result variable is verbose and error-prone.",
          "prompt": "How do switch expressions (Java 14+) improve this?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Rate.java",
              "String label = switch (level) {\n    case 1, 2 -> \"low\";\n    case 3 -> \"medium\";\n    default -> \"high\";\n};  // expression: exhaustive, no fallthrough, yields a value"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Arrow-case switch expressions yield a value, forbid fallthrough, and the compiler checks exhaustiveness",
            "Switch expressions are slower \u2014 keep the statement form for hot paths",
            "Arrow syntax only works for enums \u2014 int switches must use colons",
            "Switch expressions cannot have a default \u2014 every case must be listed"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A switch expression is an expression: it produces a value, so no mutable accumulator is needed. Arrow cases (->) do not fall through, comma-separated labels group cases, and the compiler enforces exhaustiveness (all cases covered or a default present). yield is available for colon-style blocks that compute the value.",
          "hint": "What three things does the arrow form give you that the colon form does not?",
          "objectives": [
            "Switch expressions",
            "Exhaustiveness",
            "Arrow syntax"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "java-var-limits",
          "title": "The var that will not compile",
          "situation": "A developer sprinkles var everywhere \u2014 on fields, method parameters, and a null initializer \u2014 and gets three different compile errors.",
          "prompt": "Where is var actually allowed?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Limits.java",
              "var count = 0;        // OK: local with initializer\nvar name = null;      // error: cannot infer from null\n// var as a field or parameter: error"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "var is only for local variables with an initializer the compiler can infer a type from \u2014 not fields, parameters, or null initializers",
            "var works anywhere a type appears \u2014 the errors are a compiler bug",
            "var requires the initializer to be a literal \u2014 method calls are not allowed",
            "var infers Object for null \u2014 the code compiles with a warning"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "var is not dynamic typing \u2014 it is compile-time type inference for local variables, and the compiler needs an initializer with a concrete type. Fields and parameters are part of a class's API contract, so they require explicit types. (var x = null gives the compiler nothing to infer.)",
          "hint": "What information does the compiler need to fill in for var \u2014 and where is that information missing?",
          "objectives": [
            "var",
            "Local variable type inference"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "java-records",
          "title": "The DTO with 60 lines of boilerplate",
          "situation": "A data-carrier class has final fields, a constructor, getters, equals, hashCode, and toString \u2014 all hand-written, with a copy-paste bug in equals.",
          "prompt": "Which Java 16+ feature generates all of this correctly?",
          "evidence": [
