          ],
          "choices": [
            "A record \u2014 the compiler generates the canonical constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode, and toString from the header",
            "An interface with default methods \u2014 it generates the boilerplate at runtime",
            "A sealed class \u2014 sealing triggers boilerplate generation",
            "Records are syntax sugar for HashMap \u2014 put the fields in a map instead"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "record Point(int x, int y) declares an immutable data carrier: the compiler synthesizes the constructor, x()/y() accessors, and value-based equals/hashCode/toString. Records are final (cannot be extended) and their components are final \u2014 custom validation goes in the compact constructor. Use them for DTOs, tuples, and value objects.",
          "hint": "Which declaration turns a field list directly into a complete immutable value class?",
          "objectives": [
            "Records",
            "Data carriers",
            "Compact constructors"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "java-pattern-instanceof",
          "title": "The cast after instanceof",
          "situation": "Legacy code checks instanceof then casts on the next line \u2014 verbose, and a rename once left the cast pointing at the wrong type.",
          "prompt": "How does pattern matching for instanceof (Java 16+) remove the cast?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Shape.java",
              "if (shape instanceof Circle c) {\n    System.out.println(c.radius());  // c is bound and already a Circle\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Pattern matching binds the cast variable in the same expression \u2014 no explicit cast, and the variable is in scope where the test passed",
            "instanceof now returns the cast object instead of boolean \u2014 assign it directly",
            "The pattern form is slower \u2014 keep the explicit cast in hot code",
            "Pattern variables are always nullable \u2014 add a null check after binding"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "shape instanceof Circle c tests and casts in one step, introducing c as a Circle wherever the pattern matched (flow scoping). It eliminates the duplicated type name where cast bugs hide, and the compiler rejects impossible patterns. (instanceof is false for null, so no NPE on the test.)",
          "hint": "What two things did the old code do on two lines that the pattern does on one?",
          "objectives": [
            "Pattern matching",
            "instanceof patterns"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        }
