      "id": "oop-design",
      "name": "OOP & Design",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "java-override-annotation",
          "title": "The overload that pretended to override",
          "situation": "A subclass meant to override notify(String) but declared notify(Object). The base version kept running with no compiler complaint.",
          "prompt": "Which annotation turns this silent bug into a compile error?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Alert.java",
              "class EmailAlert extends Alert {\n    public void notify(Object msg) { ... }  // overloads, does not override\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "@Override \u2014 the compiler verifies an actual overridden method exists and fails the build otherwise",
            "@Deprecated \u2014 marks the method as legacy so callers avoid it",
            "@SuppressWarnings \u2014 hides the accidental overload warning",
            "No annotation can help \u2014 rename the method to avoid confusion"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Without @Override, notify(Object) is just an overload \u2014 legal Java that silently does not participate in polymorphism. @Override asks the compiler to prove a matching supertype method exists; a signature mismatch becomes an error at the declaration. Apply it to every intended override, including interface implementations.",
          "hint": "Which annotation makes the compiler check your intent to override, rather than trusting you?",
          "objectives": [
            "@Override",
            "Overriding vs overloading"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "java-default-method",
          "title": "The interface that grew a method",
          "situation": "A library interface needs a new method, but adding an abstract method would break every existing implementation.",
          "prompt": "How do default methods evolve interfaces safely?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Repo.java",
              "interface Repository {\n    void save(Entity e);\n    default void saveAll(List<Entity> es) { es.forEach(this::save); }  // new, non-breaking\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "default methods carry an implementation in the interface \u2014 existing implementers inherit it and keep compiling",
            "Default methods are static \u2014 call them as Repository.saveAll(...)",
            "Interfaces cannot have method bodies \u2014 use an abstract class instead",
            "Mark the new method abstract and ship a migration guide"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A default method provides an implementation inside the interface, so adding one does not break existing implementers \u2014 they inherit it. Implementers may still override it. (Diamond conflicts \u2014 two interfaces providing the same default \u2014 must be resolved explicitly by the implementing class, which is the one sharp edge.)",
          "hint": "What kind of interface method can add behavior without forcing every implementer to change?",
          "objectives": [
            "Default methods",
            "Interface evolution"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "java-defensive-copy",
          "title": "The immutable class that was not",
          "situation": "An 'immutable' config class stores a Date passed by the caller. Callers mutate the Date afterward and the config silently changes.",
          "prompt": "How do you make the class genuinely immutable?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Config.java",
              "public final class Config {\n    private final Date created;  // final reference, mutable object!\n    public Config(Date d) { this.created = d; }  // caller keeps aliasing it\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Defensively copy on the way in (and on the way out of getters) \u2014 new Date(d.getTime()); better, use immutable java.time types",
            "final already guarantees immutability \u2014 the callers are misusing the API",
            "Make the field static so all instances share one canonical Date",
            "Clone is broken in Java \u2014 immutability of Dates is impossible"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "final protects the reference, not the object: a final Date field still aliases a mutable Date the caller can change. True immutability requires copying mutable inputs at construction and returning copies (or immutable views) from getters. The modern fix is avoiding legacy mutable types entirely \u2014 java.time.Instant is immutable by design.",
          "hint": "What does final lock down \u2014 the reference, or the object it points to?",
          "objectives": [
            "Immutability",
            "Defensive copying",
            "java.time"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "java-equals-hashcode",
          "title": "The key that cannot be found",
          "situation": "A class overrides equals but not hashCode. Equal instances stored as HashMap keys cannot be retrieved.",
          "prompt": "Why does the map lose entries with equal keys?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Key.java",
              "class Key {\n    String id;\n    public boolean equals(Object o) { ... }  // no hashCode override!\n}\nmap.put(new Key(\"a\"), 1);\nmap.get(new Key(\"a\"));  // null"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "HashMap buckets by hashCode first \u2014 equal objects with different identity hash codes land in different buckets, so equals is never consulted; always override hashCode with equals",
            "HashMap uses == for keys \u2014 equals is irrelevant to maps",
            "The map needs a custom Comparator \u2014 equals/hashCode are for sets only",
            "String ids are interned \u2014 the bug is the new Key allocation"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Hash-based collections hash first and compare with equals only within a bucket. The contract is: equal objects must have equal hash codes. Overriding equals without hashCode leaves the default identity hash, so logically-equal keys scatter across buckets and lookups miss. IDEs generate both together for exactly this reason.",
          "hint": "What does a HashMap check before it ever calls equals?",
          "objectives": [
            "equals/hashCode contract",
            "HashMap"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "java-sealed",
          "title": "The hierarchy that must stay closed",
          "situation": "A payment-type hierarchy must allow exactly Card, Bank, and Crypto \u2014 no surprise subclasses from other teams that break an exhaustive switch.",
          "prompt": "How do sealed classes (Java 17+) enforce a closed hierarchy?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Pay.java",
              "public sealed interface Payment permits Card, Bank, Crypto { }\n// any other implementer: compile error"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "sealed with permits lists every allowed subtype \u2014 the compiler rejects all others, and switches over the hierarchy can be exhaustive",
            "Sealed classes cannot be implemented at all \u2014 use an enum instead",
            "permits is documentation only \u2014 reflection can still add subtypes",
            "Final classes already prevent extension \u2014 sealed adds nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "sealed declares a closed set of permitted direct subtypes; anything else is a compile error. Each permitted subtype must itself be final, sealed, or non-sealed (explicit choice). Combined with pattern-matching switch, the compiler can verify exhaustiveness \u2014 adding a new payment type breaks the switch loudly instead of silently.",
          "hint": "Which modifier lets a type name exactly who may extend or implement it?",
          "objectives": [
            "Sealed classes",
            "permits",
            "Exhaustive hierarchies"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
