            "Abstract classes are faster \u2014 always prefer them for performance",
            "Interfaces cannot have fields \u2014 use abstract classes whenever data is involved",
            "There is no difference since Java 8 default methods \u2014 pick randomly"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A class extends exactly one class but implements many interfaces, so capabilities (Flyable, Swimmable) belong in interfaces while a true is-a backbone with shared state and constructor logic belongs in an abstract class. Default methods narrowed the gap, but state, constructors, and single-inheritance still distinguish them.",
          "hint": "How many of each can one class have \u2014 and what does that imply for modeling capabilities versus identity?",
