          "id": "java-pecs",
          "title": "The producer list you cannot add to",
          "situation": "A method accepts List<? extends Number> to read numbers, but a teammate tries to add an Integer and the compiler rejects it.",
          "prompt": "Why does the wildcard bound forbid adding?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Stats.java",
              "double sum(List<? extends Number> ns) {\n    // ns.add(1);  // error: cannot add to a producer\n    double s = 0; for (Number n : ns) s += n.doubleValue(); return s;\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "PECS: ? extends Number is a producer \u2014 the list could be List<Double>, so adding an Integer would corrupt it; use ? super Integer for consumers",
            "Wildcard lists are immutable \u2014 wrap with Collections.unmodifiableList first",
            "The compiler cannot verify autoboxing through wildcards \u2014 add ((Number)(Integer)1)",
            "? extends Number means List<Number> exactly \u2014 the caller's List<Integer> should not compile"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "? extends Number means 'a List of some specific subtype of Number, we know not which' \u2014 it could be List<Double>, where an Integer does not belong. The compiler therefore forbids adds (except null). PECS: producer-extends (read from it), consumer-super (write to it \u2014 List<? super Integer> accepts Integer adds).",
          "hint": "If the actual list is a List<Double>, what goes wrong when someone adds an Integer through your reference?",
          "objectives": [
