        {
          "id": "java-stream-lazy",
          "title": "The pipeline that never runs",
          "situation": "A stream pipeline with filter and map produces no output and the mapped function's side effects never happen.",
          "prompt": "Why does nothing execute?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "Pipe.java",
              "orders.stream()\n    .filter(o -> o.total() > 100)\n    .map(o -> { audit(o); return o; });  // no terminal op: nothing runs"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Intermediate operations are lazy \u2014 without a terminal operation (forEach, collect, count) the pipeline never executes",
            "Streams are single-threaded \u2014 the pipeline needs .parallel() to run",
            "map with a block lambda is illegal \u2014 use a method reference",
            "filter short-circuits the stream \u2014 remove the filter to see output"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "filter/map/peek are intermediate: they only describe the pipeline. Execution is triggered by a terminal operation like forEach, collect, reduce, or count. This laziness enables fusion and short-circuiting (findFirst stops early), but a pipeline with no terminal op is dead code. (Also: side effects belong in forEach/peek for debugging, not in map.)",
