  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "language-core",
      "title": "Language Core \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "String literals are interned (one shared instance per distinct literal), so == can appear to work in tests, but a runtime-built string is a different object with equal content. equals() compares content \u2014 always use it for value comparison.",
        "equals vs ==: (For null-safety, \"admin\".equals(input) avoids NPE.).",
        "Integer division: promoting one operand to double before dividing (done * 100.0 / total) performs floating-point division; cast back to int for display.",
        "Switch expressions: a switch expression is an expression: it produces a value, so no mutable accumulator is needed."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "String input = scanner.nextLine();\nif (input == \"admin\") { grant(); }  // false for runtime strings",
        "walkthrough": "== on objects tests reference identity. String literals are interned (one shared instance per distinct literal), so == can appear to work in tests, but a runtime-built string is a different object with equal content. equals() compares content \u2014 always use it for value comparison."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: var works anywhere a type appears.",
        "Common trap: arrow syntax only works for enums.",
        "Common trap: switch expressions cannot have a default.",
        "Common trap: records are syntax sugar for HashMap."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "oop-design",
      "title": "OOP & Design \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "@Override: without @Override, notify(Object) is just an overload \u2014 legal Java that silently does not participate in polymorphism. @Override asks the compiler to prove a matching supertype method exists; a signature mismatch becomes an error at the declaration.",
        "Overriding equals without hashCode leaves the default identity hash, so logically-equal keys scatter across buckets and lookups miss.",
        "Default methods narrowed the gap, but state, constructors, and single-inheritance still distinguish them. A default method provides an implementation inside the interface, so adding one does not break existing implementers \u2014 they inherit it.",
        "Interface evolution: apply it to every intended override, including interface implementations.",
        "Immutability: true immutability requires copying mutable inputs at construction and returning copies (or immutable views) from getters."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "public sealed interface Payment permits Card, Bank, Crypto { }\n// any other implementer: compile error",
        "walkthrough": "sealed declares a closed set of permitted direct subtypes; anything else is a compile error. Each permitted subtype must itself be final, sealed, or non-sealed (explicit choice). Combined with pattern-matching switch, the compiler can verify exhaustiveness \u2014 adding a new payment type breaks the switch loudly instead of silently."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: use abstract classes whenever data is involved.",
        "Common trap: there is no difference since Java 8 default methods.",
        "Common trap: sealed classes cannot be implemented at all.",
        "Common trap: mark the new method abstract and ship a migration guide."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "collections-generics",
      "title": "Collections & Generics \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Type erasure: generics are erased at compile time: at runtime a Cache<String> is just Cache, so the JVM could not enforce new T[]'s type safety (arrays are covariant and reified, generics are invariant and erased \u2014 the combination is unsound).",
        "PECS: producer-extends (read from it), consumer-super (write to it \u2014 List<? super Integer> accepts Integer adds).",
        "Producer extends: ? extends Number means 'a List of some specific subtype of Number, we know not which' \u2014 it could be List<Double>, where an Integer does not belong."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "List<String> tags = List.of(\"a\", \"b\");\ntags.add(\"c\");  // UnsupportedOperationException",
        "walkthrough": "The List.of/Set.of/Map.of factories (Java 9+) return structurally immutable collections \u2014 any mutator throws UnsupportedOperationException. They also reject null elements outright. When callers need to mutate, copy into an ArrayList (or collect with Collectors.toList(), whose mutability is the implementation's documented choice)."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: arrays of generics are allowed since Java 10.",
        "Common trap: the compiler cannot verify autoboxing through wildcards.",
        "Common trap: ? extends Number means List<Number> exactly.",
        "Common trap: list.of returns null for empty input."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "streams-lambdas",
      "title": "Streams & Lambdas \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Lazy evaluation: (Also note orElse evaluates its argument eagerly \u2014 orElseGet takes a Supplier for lazy defaults.).",
        "Intermediate vs terminal ops: execution is triggered by a terminal operation like forEach, collect, reduce, or count.",
        "Effectively final: java enforces this by requiring captured locals to be final or effectively final (never reassigned).",
        "Lambdas capture local variables by value (a copy), so allowing later mutation would create two divergent truths. A lambda that does nothing but call a method (s -> s.toUpperCase()) is exactly a method reference (String::toUpperCase).",
        "Optional is a container signaling 'value or absence' \u2014 calling get() without checking reintroduces the exact null-dereference-style crash it replaces.",
        "orElse vs orElseGet: the API's combinators (map, flatMap, filter, orElse, orElseGet, orElseThrow) handle both cases declaratively."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "Optional<User> u = repo.find(id);\nreturn u.get().name();  // throws when empty",
        "walkthrough": "Optional is a container signaling 'value or absence' \u2014 calling get() without checking reintroduces the exact null-dereference-style crash it replaces. The API's combinators (map, flatMap, filter, orElse, orElseGet, orElseThrow) handle both cases declaratively."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: method references cannot take arguments.",
        "Common trap: forEachOrdered fixes thread safety.",
        "Common trap: wrap the collect in try/catch and retry with a HashMap.",
        "Common trap: the pipeline needs .parallel() to run."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "concurrency-virtual-threads",
      "title": "Concurrency & Virtual Threads \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Race conditions: deadlock needs four conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait).",
        "AtomicInteger uses CAS (compare-and-set) hardware instructions to make the whole read-modify-write indivisible. (For read-modify-write like counters, volatile is not enough \u2014 that needs atomicity, i.e., AtomicInteger.).",
        "Lost updates: hits++ compiles to load, increment, store \u2014 between any two steps another thread can interleave and its update gets overwritten (lost update).",
        "Lock ordering: a global ordering \u2014 e.g., always lock the account with the smaller id first \u2014 makes cycles impossible."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "// thread 1: synchronized(a) { synchronized(b) { transfer(a, b); } }\n// thread 2: synchronized(b) { synchronized(a) { transfer(b, a); } }  // deadlock",
        "walkthrough": "Deadlock needs four conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait). Acquiring locks in opposite orders creates the circular wait. Alternatives: tryLock with timeout and backoff, or a single coarse lock when contention is low."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: virtual threads share one ThreadLocal map.",
        "Common trap: blocking calls are forbidden on virtual threads.",
        "Common trap: threadLocal is deprecated for virtual threads.",
        "Common trap: virtual threads eliminate the need for executors."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "modules-jars-io",
      "title": "Modules, JARs & I/O \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "JPMS: the module system (JPMS) enforces strong encapsulation: a public class in a non-exported package is inaccessible outside its module, even via reflection (without opens). exports declares the module's public API surface; everything else is implementation detail.",
        "Executable JARs: (Fat/uber JARs additionally unpack dependencies into the single archive.).",
        "Manifest: java -jar locates the entry point through the JAR manifest's Main-Class attribute (META-INF/MANIFEST.MF)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "try (var in = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) {\n    return in.lines().filter(...).toList();\n}  // in.close() automatic, even on exception",
        "walkthrough": "try-with-resources closes each resource in reverse declaration order when the block exits \u2014 normally, via return, or by exception. If both the body and close() throw, the close exception is suppressed (retrievable via getSuppressed()) rather than masking the original. Any class implementing AutoCloseable participates, including custom resources."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: all public classes are visible across modules.",
        "Common trap: closeable resources are closed by the garbage collector.",
        "Common trap: jARs cannot contain properties files.",
        "Common trap: declare the reader outside try and close it after the catch blocks."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "language-core",
      "title": "Java \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "The JVM runs bytecode: javac compiles .java to .class, and the JVM (with its JIT compiler) executes it \u2014 'write once, run anywhere' holds where a JVM exists. The garbage collector reclaims unreachable objects, so you manage reachability (nulling references, closing resources) rather than freeing memory.",
        "The collections framework separates interface from implementation: List (ArrayList for random access, LinkedList for deque operations), Set (HashSet, TreeSet), Map (HashMap, TreeMap). Choosing correctly starts from the access pattern, and the equals/hashCode contract is load-bearing for every hash-based collection.",
        "Streams express data pipelines declaratively: intermediate operations (filter, map, flatMap) are lazy and fuse into a single pass; terminal operations (collect, reduce, forEach) trigger execution. Parallel streams exist but only pay off for large, CPU-bound, side-effect-free pipelines.",
        "Concurrency uses threads, but raw Thread management is mostly obsolete: ExecutorService pools (fixed, cached, scheduled) own thread lifecycle, Futures/CompletableFuture compose async results, and java.util.concurrent provides the building blocks (ConcurrentHashMap, BlockingQueue, CountDownLatch). Always shut down your executors.",
        "The Java Memory Model governs cross-thread visibility: without synchronization (synchronized, volatile, locks, concurrent collections) there is no guarantee one thread sees another's writes. Immutable objects and safe publication are the simplest correct patterns.",
        "Spring Boot is convention-over-configuration: starters pull in coherent dependency sets, auto-configuration (@ConditionalOn...) wires beans from the classpath and properties, and an embedded server (Tomcat/Jetty) means `java -jar app.jar` is the whole deployment. Externalize config per environment \u2014 never hardcode URLs or secrets."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "java",
        "code": "import java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.*;\n\nrecord Order(String id, double total) {}\n\nMap<String, Double> bigSpenders(List<Order> orders) {\n    return orders.stream()\n        .filter(o -> o.total() > 100)          // lazy\n        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(       // terminal: triggers\n            Order::id,\n            Collectors.summingDouble(Order::total))); // per-id totals\n}",
        "walkthrough": "filter is lazy \u2014 nothing runs until collect pulls elements through. groupingBy with summingDouble aggregates per order id in one pass. Records give you an immutable value type with correct equals/hashCode for free."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: overriding equals without hashCode \u2014 hash-based collections silently lose your entries.",
        "Common trap: calling a stream pipeline without a terminal operation \u2014 lazy means nothing executes.",
        "Common trap: modifying a collection during for-each iteration \u2014 fail-fast iterators throw even single-threaded.",
        "Common trap: forgetting executor shutdown \u2014 non-daemon pool threads keep the JVM alive forever."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "java",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/java"
    },
    {
      "title": "From Static Diagrams to Living Systems",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "A textbook diagram shows you one part in isolation \u2014 a living systems view shows you how all parts of Java move together in sequence",
        "The four-step systems anatomy method: (1) See the whole in context, (2) Cut it open to reveal layers, (3) Walk through the process step-by-step, (4) Pull apart into labeled pieces",
        "Apply this to any system in this course: start with the trigger action, trace through every component it touches, watch the data flow",
        "Interview/exam power: when asked 'how does X work?', narrate the journey \u2014 'it starts here, then...'. This demonstrates senior-level systems thinking"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "TRIGGER event occurs\n  \u2192 First system receives it (see the handoff)\n  \u2192 Processing happens in sequence (watch each step)\n  \u2192 State changes propagate (follow the ripple)\n  \u2192 Final outcome delivered\n\nEach arrow is a living handoff, not a static box.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the 'jet engine cutaway' for your field: instead of memorizing isolated facts, you see the entire flow as a moving sequence. Practice narrating this journey out loud."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Memorizing terms without understanding the processes they participate in",
        "Drawing architecture diagrams as boxes without showing the data flow between them",
        "Answering 'how does X work?' with a definition instead of walking through the sequence"
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any process in this course. Write out the full journey from trigger to outcome, naming every system touched."
    },
