  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "Java SE \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Language Core",
          "items": [
            "String literals \u2014 are interned (one shared instance per distinct literal), so == can appear to work in tests, but a runtime-built string is a different object with equal content. equals() compares content \u2014 always use it for value comparison.",
            "Integer division \u2014 promoting one operand to double before dividing (done * 100.0 / total) performs floating-point division; cast back to int for display.",
            "Switch expressions \u2014 a switch expression is an expression: it produces a value, so no mutable accumulator is needed.",
            "Common trap \u2014 var works anywhere a type appears.",
            "Common trap \u2014 arrow syntax only works for enums.",
            "Common trap \u2014 switch expressions cannot have a default.",
            "Common trap \u2014 records are syntax sugar for HashMap."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "OOP & Design",
          "items": [
            "Overriding \u2014 Overriding equals without hashCode leaves the default identity hash, so logically-equal keys scatter across buckets and lookups miss.",
            "Default \u2014 Default methods narrowed the gap, but state, constructors, and single-inheritance still distinguish them. A default method provides an implementation inside the interface, so adding one does not break existing implementers \u2014 they inherit it.",
            "Interface evolution \u2014 apply it to every intended override, including interface implementations.",
            "Immutability \u2014 true immutability requires copying mutable inputs at construction and returning copies (or immutable views) from getters.",
            "Common trap \u2014 use abstract classes whenever data is involved.",
            "Common trap \u2014 there is no difference since Java 8 default methods.",
            "Common trap \u2014 sealed classes cannot be implemented at all.",
            "Common trap \u2014 mark the new method abstract and ship a migration guide."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Collections & Generics",
          "items": [
            "Type erasure \u2014 generics are erased at compile time: at runtime a Cache<String> is just Cache, so the JVM could not enforce new T[]'s type safety (arrays are covariant and reified, generics are invariant and erased \u2014 the combination is unsound).",
            "PECS \u2014 producer-extends (read from it), consumer-super (write to it \u2014 List<? super Integer> accepts Integer adds).",
            "Producer extends \u2014 ? extends Number means 'a List of some specific subtype of Number, we know not which' \u2014 it could be List<Double>, where an Integer does not belong.",
            "Common trap \u2014 arrays of generics are allowed since Java 10.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the compiler cannot verify autoboxing through wildcards.",
            "Common trap \u2014 ? extends Number means List<Number> exactly.",
            "Common trap \u2014 list.of returns null for empty input.",
            "The List.of \u2014 The List.of/Set.of/Map.of factories (Java 9+) return structurally immutable collections \u2014 any mutator throws UnsupportedOperationException. They also reject null elements outright. When callers need to mutate, copy into an ArrayList (or collect with Collect\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Streams & Lambdas",
          "items": [
            "Lazy evaluation \u2014 (Also note orElse evaluates its argument eagerly \u2014 orElseGet takes a Supplier for lazy defaults.).",
            "Intermediate vs terminal ops \u2014 execution is triggered by a terminal operation like forEach, collect, reduce, or count.",
            "Effectively final \u2014 java enforces this by requiring captured locals to be final or effectively final (never reassigned).",
            "Lambdas \u2014 Lambdas capture local variables by value (a copy), so allowing later mutation would create two divergent truths. A lambda that does nothing but call a method (s -> s.toUpperCase()) is exactly a method reference (String::toUpperCase).",
            "Optional is a container signaling 'value or absence' \u2014 calling get() without checking reintroduces the exact null-dereference-style crash it replaces.",
            "Common trap \u2014 method references cannot take arguments.",
            "Common trap \u2014 forEachOrdered fixes thread safety.",
            "Common trap \u2014 wrap the collect in try/catch and retry with a HashMap.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the pipeline needs .parallel() to run.",
            "Optional is a container signaling 'value or absence' \u2014 calling get() without checking reintroduces the exact null-dereference-style crash it replaces. The API's combinators (map, flatMap, filter, orElse, orElseGet, orElseThrow) handle both cases declaratively."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Concurrency & Virtual Threads",
          "items": [
            "Race conditions \u2014 deadlock needs four conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait).",
            "AtomicInteger \u2014 uses CAS (compare-and-set) hardware instructions to make the whole read-modify-write indivisible. (For read-modify-write like counters, volatile is not enough \u2014 that needs atomicity, i.e., AtomicInteger.).",
            "Lost updates \u2014 hits++ compiles to load, increment, store \u2014 between any two steps another thread can interleave and its update gets overwritten (lost update).",
            "Lock ordering \u2014 a global ordering \u2014 e.g., always lock the account with the smaller id first \u2014 makes cycles impossible.",
            "Common trap \u2014 virtual threads share one ThreadLocal map.",
            "Common trap \u2014 blocking calls are forbidden on virtual threads.",
            "Common trap \u2014 threadLocal is deprecated for virtual threads.",
            "Common trap \u2014 virtual threads eliminate the need for executors.",
            "Deadlock \u2014 Deadlock needs four conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait). Acquiring locks in opposite orders creates the circular wait. Alternatives: tryLock with timeout and backoff, or a single coarse lock when contention is low."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Modules, JARs & I/O",
          "items": [
            "JPMS \u2014 the module system (JPMS) enforces strong encapsulation: a public class in a non-exported package is inaccessible outside its module, even via reflection (without opens). exports declares the module's public API surface; everything else is implementation det\u2026",
            "Executable JARs \u2014 (Fat/uber JARs additionally unpack dependencies into the single archive.).",
            "Manifest \u2014 java -jar locates the entry point through the JAR manifest's Main-Class attribute (META-INF/MANIFEST.MF).",
            "Common trap \u2014 all public classes are visible across modules.",
            "Common trap \u2014 closeable resources are closed by the garbage collector.",
            "Common trap \u2014 jARs cannot contain properties files.",
            "Common trap \u2014 declare the reader outside try and close it after the catch blocks."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "API",
        "def": "JPMS: the module system (JPMS) enforces strong encapsulation: a public class in a non-exported package is inaccessible outside its module, even via reflection (without opens). exports declares the module's public API surface; everything\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Java",
        "def": "final protects the reference, not the object: a final Date field still aliases a mutable Date the caller can change. True immutability requires copying mutable inputs at construction and returning copies (or immutable views) from getters."
      },
      {
        "term": "JVM",
        "def": "What keeps a JVM alive after main returns \u2014 and whose threads are those?"
      },
      {
        "term": "JAR",
        "def": "The module system (JPMS) enforces strong encapsulation: a public class in a non-exported package is inaccessible outside its module, even via reflection (without opens). exports declares the module's public API surface; everything else i\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "String",
        "def": "String literals are interned (one shared instance per distinct literal), so == can appear to work in tests, but a runtime-built string is a different object with equal content. equals() compares content \u2014 always use it for value comparison."
      },
      {
        "term": "List",
        "def": "If the actual list is a List<Double>, what goes wrong when someone adds an Integer through your reference?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Integer",
        "def": "A lambda that does nothing but call a method (s -> s.toUpperCase()) is exactly a method reference (String::toUpperCase). The four forms: static (Integer::parseInt), bound instance (System.out::println), unbound instance (String::length \u2014\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "ArrayList",
        "def": "A lambda that does nothing but call a method (s -> s.toUpperCase()) is exactly a method reference (String::toUpperCase). The four forms: static (Integer::parseInt), bound instance (System.out::println), unbound instance (String::length \u2014\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "How",
        "def": "How does a TreeSet decide two elements are the same \u2014 equals(), or something else?"
      },
      {
        "term": "PECS",
        "def": "PECS: producer-extends (read from it), consumer-super (write to it \u2014 List<? super Integer> accepts Integer adds)."
      },
      {
        "term": "JPMS",
        "def": "JPMS: the module system (JPMS) enforces strong encapsulation: a public class in a non-exported package is inaccessible outside its module, even via reflection (without opens). exports declares the module's public API surface; everything\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "AtomicInteger",
        "def": "AtomicInteger uses CAS (compare-and-set) hardware instructions to make the whole read-modify-write indivisible. (For read-modify-write like counters, volatile is not enough \u2014 that needs atomicity, i.e., AtomicInteger.)."
      },
      {
        "term": "Object",
        "def": "@Override: without @Override, notify(Object) is just an overload \u2014 legal Java that silently does not participate in polymorphism. @Override asks the compiler to prove a matching supertype method exists; a signature mismatch becomes an er\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Number",
        "def": "Producer extends: ? extends Number means 'a List of some specific subtype of Number, we know not which' \u2014 it could be List<Double>, where an Integer does not belong."
      },
      {
        "term": "Without",
        "def": "Without @Override, notify(Object) is just an overload \u2014 legal Java that silently does not participate in polymorphism. @Override asks the compiler to prove a matching supertype method exists; a signature mismatch becomes an error at the\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Optional",
        "def": "Optional is a container signaling 'value or absence' \u2014 calling get() without checking reintroduces the exact null-dereference-style crash it replaces."
      },
      {
        "term": "Override",
        "def": "@Override: without @Override, notify(Object) is just an overload \u2014 legal Java that silently does not participate in polymorphism. @Override asks the compiler to prove a matching supertype method exists; a signature mismatch becomes an er\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Default",
        "def": "Default methods narrowed the gap, but state, constructors, and single-inheritance still distinguish them. A default method provides an implementation inside the interface, so adding one does not break existing implementers \u2014 they inherit\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Cache",
        "def": "Type erasure: generics are erased at compile time: at runtime a Cache<String> is just Cache, so the JVM could not enforce new T[]'s type safety (arrays are covariant and reified, generics are invariant and erased \u2014 the combination is uns\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Virtual",
        "def": "Platform threads are expensive (~MB stacks, OS threads), so blocking I/O on them wastes a scarce resource \u2014 hence pool sizing. Virtual threads are JVM-managed and cheap; when one blocks, its carrier thread is freed for another virtual th\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Alternatives",
        "def": "Deadlock needs four conditions (mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait). Acquiring locks in opposite orders creates the circular wait."
      },
      {
        "term": "OS",
        "def": "new File(...) addresses the OS filesystem relative to the working directory \u2014 inside a JAR there is no config/app.properties file on disk. Classpath resources (getResource/getResourceAsStream) are resolved by the class loader, which read\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Fat/uber JARs",
        "def": "Executable JARs: (Fat/uber JARs additionally unpack dependencies into the single archive.)."
      },
      {
        "term": "Immutability",
        "def": "Immutability: true immutability requires copying mutable inputs at construction and returning copies (or immutable views) from getters."
      },
      {
        "term": "Who",
        "def": "Who is allowed to structurally modify a collection mid-iteration \u2014 the loop body directly, or someone else?"
      }
    ],
